<section class="page" id="p-pcr">
  <h1>PCR Activities</h1>

  <div class="summary">
    <h2>Final Reflection: How College Developed Me Professionally</h2>
    <p>I came into college already used to responsibility. Being an Eagle Scout and a Head Lifeguard meant I knew how to lead a team and keep my head in a stressful situation. WWU taught me how to turn that instinct into professional skill. In MIS, I learned to look at a business problem and ask what data would actually answer it, then build something that gets that answer to the people making the decision. My PCR activities pushed me past the classroom: I practiced presenting to an audience, wrote applications for real internships, and thought harder about who our systems leave out. The biggest change is that I now think about the person on the other end of every project, whether that's a hiring manager reading my cover letter or a student checking RecSync before heading to the gym. Over time I've also gotten more comfortable with feedback, because each revision of my resume, presentations, and code came out better than the one before. In my career I plan to work as the bridge between technical teams and business leaders, and to bring the same mix of preparation, clear communication, and accountability that I learned on the pool deck.</p>
  </div>

  <p class="soon" style="margin:26px 0 0">Point totals coming soon.</p>
