    <div class="act">
      <h3>AI Ethics Analysis: Waymo &amp; Uber Case Studies <span class="pts">Points coming soon</span></h3>
      <div class="meta">Date: Fall 2026</div>
      <p>In MIS 432 this fall, I analyzed how Waymo and Uber use AI to make decisions that affect real people, from autonomous driving to surge pricing. Working through these cases forced me to think about who carries the risk when a model gets something wrong. I realized that a system can be technically accurate and still be unfair or unsafe in ways that never show up in an accuracy number. I liked that the assignments asked for a recommendation and not just a summary, because I had to take a position and defend it. The biggest skill I gained was asking "what happens to the person on the other end?" before asking "does the model work?" I also learned that being transparent about a system's limits is part of acting professionally. When I build or recommend AI tools in my career, I plan to put those ethical tradeoffs in front of decision-makers instead of burying them in the technical details.</p>
    </div>
  </div>

