  <!-- ---------- COMMUNICATION ---------- -->
  <div class="cat">
    <h2>Professional Communication</h2>

    <div class="act">
      <h3>AI Value Presentation, MIS 432 <span class="pts">Points coming soon</span></h3>
      <div class="meta">Date: Fall 2026</div>
      <p>This fall I built and delivered a presentation in MIS 432 on how AI creates business value. I paired an interview with Amr Awadallah of Vectara on retrieval-augmented generation with a news article on Demis Hassabis. I organized the talk around the Data → Model → Prediction → Decision → Value framework, so the audience could follow how a technical idea turns into a business result. The hardest part was cutting technical detail without losing the point, which is a real skill when presenting to non-technical people. I liked having a clear framework, because it gave the talk a backbone and kept me from rambling. Presenting helped me get more comfortable speaking in front of a room and handling questions I hadn't prepared for. In my career I expect to explain technical systems to managers and clients regularly, and this taught me to lead with the decision and value before getting into the model.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="act">
      <h3>RecSync Business Plan <span class="pts">Points coming soon</span></h3>
      <div class="meta">Date: Coming soon</div>
      <p>I wrote a full business plan for RecSync, a system that forecasts traffic at the WWU Recreation Center and recommends the best times for students to work out. I framed it with the AI Factory model to show how data from the Rec Center could feed predictions that improve the student experience. Writing a plan for an audience that might actually fund or adopt the idea made me much more precise than a typical class paper would. I had to explain the problem, the solution, and the value in plain language, and I had to anticipate objections. I liked that it was my own idea, which made me care about getting the communication right. The project strengthened my persuasive writing and my ability to organize a complex proposal so it's easy to scan. When I pitch ideas or write proposals at work, I'll use the same approach: start with the user's problem and back every claim with a clear reason.</p>
    </div>

    <div class="act">
